<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bolesław I the Brave</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">967–1025</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Bolesław Chrobry, Boleslaw I the Brave</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bolesław I the Brave was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> from 992 and its first crowned king in 1025. Son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a>, he hosted Emperor Otto III at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a> in 1000, when an archbishopric was established there. He fought long wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> and Milsko, and in 1018 briefly captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, making Poland a major Central European power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boleslaw-i-the-brave.jpg" alt="Bolesław I the Brave" width="320" height="375" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mathiasrex, Maciej Szczepańczyk · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boles%C5%82aw_I_the_Brave_buys_the_corpse_of_Saint_Adalbert_of_Prague_from_the_Prussians,_Gniezno_Door_ca._1170.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
